# Behavioural tests: calling conventions between qc-- and gcc.
#
# Ported from TODO/tests/quest (originally tests/quest/mkfile + Quest, a
# random test-case generator: https://github.com/nrnrnr/qc--'s quest tool).
# Each test-N pair was generated by Quest and pre-translated to C-- by LCC
# upstream, long before this fork - neither Quest nor LCC needs to run
# again, the .c/.c-- pairs are just checked in. N is Quest's complexity
# knob: test-0 is scalars only, test-1/2 add structs passed and returned by
# value. See quest/README for the original mkfile-driven recipe this
# replaces.
#
# Each test compiles test-N-main and test-N-callee, one half with qc and
# the other with gcc, links them together, and runs the result: the callee
# checks the arguments it was passed against known values and asserts on
# its own return value, so a passing run is silent and exits 0. This is a
# pure ABI test - no C-- globals, no runtime library, no pcmap linking - so
# unlike run-tiger.sh/run-rt.sh, run-quest.sh does not build/link
# runtime/build-x86/libqcmm.a at all.
#
# Both directions of the pair matter independently: cmm-gcc catches qc--
# argument-passing bugs (it emits the call), gcc-cmm catches qc--
# parameter-receiving bugs (it emits the callee prologue).
#
# Columns:  name  num  dir  expected-rc
#   num: the Quest complexity number (0, 1, 2)
#   dir: cmm-gcc (main compiled by qc, callee by gcc)
#        gcc-cmm (main compiled by gcc, callee compiled by qc)
# expected-rc is always 0 (the ABI-correct answer - the callee's exit code
# is its count of failed assertions); some of these currently do not reach
# it (see expected/quest.txt) - qc's x86 backend does not yet implement the
# "C returns struct" calling convention that test-1/2's callees need.

test-0-cmm-gcc  0  cmm-gcc  0
test-0-gcc-cmm  0  gcc-cmm  0
test-1-cmm-gcc  1  cmm-gcc  0
test-1-gcc-cmm  1  gcc-cmm  0
test-2-cmm-gcc  2  cmm-gcc  0
test-2-gcc-cmm  2  gcc-cmm  0
